🧠 Interview Question
What happens if you use map() instead?
list.stream()
    .map(List::stream)

The result is:

Stream<Stream<Integer>>

Not what we want.

With flatMap():
list.stream()
    .flatMap(List::stream)

The result is:

Stream<Integer>

That's why it's called flatMap—it flattens nested streams into a single stream.

⭐ Remember Forever
map()
List<String>
        ↓
Stream<String>
        ↓ map(String::length)
Stream<Integer>

One input → One output.

flatMap()
List<List<Integer>>
        ↓
Stream<List<Integer>>
        ↓ flatMap(List::stream)
Stream<Integer>

One input → Many outputs, then flatten into one stream.


